============================================================ QUESTION 4
– PYTHON AUTOMATED TESTING USING PYTEST
============================================================

Aim: Create a Python application and automate its testing using Pytest.

Where to run: Killercoda terminal, inside the repository’s set3/ folder.

A. Enter the Set 3 folder:

    cd /root/set3-devops/devops-external1/set3

B. Create app.py:

    cat > app.py <<'EOF'
    def add(a, b):
        return a + b

    def subtract(a, b):
        return a - b

    def multiply(a, b):
        return a * b

    def divide(a, b):
        if b == 0:
            raise ValueError("Cannot divide by zero")
        return a / b
    EOF

C. Create test_app.py:

    cat > test_app.py <<'EOF'
    import pytest
    from app import add, subtract, multiply, divide

    def test_add():
        assert add(10, 5) == 15

    def test_subtract():
        assert subtract(10, 5) == 5

    def test_multiply():
        assert multiply(10, 5) == 50

    def test_divide():
        assert divide(10, 5) == 2

    def test_divide_by_zero():
        with pytest.raises(ValueError):
            divide(10, 0)
    EOF

D. Create requirements.txt:

    echo "pytest" > requirements.txt

E. Create .gitignore so the virtual environment is not uploaded:

    cat > .gitignore <<'EOF'
    venv/
    __pycache__/
    .pytest_cache/
    *.pyc
    test-results.xml
    EOF

F. Create and activate virtual environment:

    python3 -m venv venv
    source venv/bin/activate

If python3 -m venv venv reports that ensurepip/venv is unavailable,
install the venv package first, then retry:

    apt-get update
    apt-get install -y python3.12-venv
    rm -rf venv
    python3 -m venv venv
    source venv/bin/activate

G. Install dependencies:

    pip install --upgrade pip
    pip install -r requirements.txt

H. Run automated tests:

    pytest -v

Expected result:

    test_app.py::test_add PASSED
    test_app.py::test_subtract PASSED
    test_app.py::test_multiply PASSED
    test_app.py::test_divide PASSED
    test_app.py::test_divide_by_zero PASSED

    5 passed

I. Optional JUnit-style test report:

    pytest -v --junitxml=test-results.xml

Question 4 is complete when all five tests pass.

============================================================ QUESTION 1
– JENKINS FREESTYLE JOB
============================================================

Aim: Create a Jenkins Freestyle Job that obtains source code from GitHub
and runs the Python automated tests.

PART A – Create the build script

Where to run: Killercoda terminal, inside the repository’s set3/ folder.

    cd /root/set3-devops/devops-external1/set3

Create freestyle-build.sh:

    cat > freestyle-build.sh <<'EOF'
    #!/bin/bash
    set -e

    echo "Starting Jenkins Freestyle Job"

    python3 -m venv venv
    source venv/bin/activate

    pip install --upgrade pip
    pip install -r requirements.txt

    echo "Running Python Tests"
    pytest -v

    echo "Jenkins Freestyle Job Completed Successfully"
    EOF

Make executable:

    chmod +x freestyle-build.sh

Check files:

    ls -la

The folder should contain: app.py test_app.py requirements.txt
freestyle-build.sh .gitignore

PART B – Commit and push Set 3 files

Where to run: Killercoda terminal, from repository root.

    cd /root/set3-devops/devops-external1

Configure Git identity once (use your own GitHub email):

    git config --global user.name "YOUR_GITHUB_USERNAME"
    git config --global user.email "YOUR_GITHUB_EMAIL"

Add and commit:

    git add set3
    git commit -m "Add Set 3 Python application and tests"
    git push origin main

If Git says “nothing to commit”, check git status; the files may already
be committed. Confirm the files are visible in the GitHub repository.

PART C – Start Jenkins in Killercoda

Check Docker:

    docker --version
    docker info

Start Jenkins only if it is not already running:

    docker ps

If a Jenkins container is already running, do not create a second
container. If it is not running, use:

    docker run -d \
      --name jenkins \
      -p 8080:8080 \
      -p 50000:50000 \
      -u root \
      -v jenkins_home:/var/jenkins_home \
      jenkins/jenkins:lts

Check:

    docker ps
    docker logs -f jenkins

When Jenkins has finished starting, press Ctrl+C to leave the log
display. This does not stop the Jenkins container.

Install Git and Python inside the Jenkins container:

    docker exec -u root jenkins bash -c \
    "apt-get update && apt-get install -y git python3 python3-venv python3-pip"

Check:

    docker exec jenkins git --version
    docker exec jenkins python3 --version

Get the initial Jenkins administrator password:

    docker exec jenkins cat /var/jenkins_home/secrets/initialAdminPassword

Run this command in a Killercoda terminal tab, NOT in the Jenkins
browser. Copy the displayed password and paste it into the Jenkins
Unlock page.

PART D – Open Jenkins

Do NOT open http://localhost:8080 on your laptop. Jenkins is running
inside the remote Killercoda environment.

In Killercoda, use the exposed-port / Traffic / Ports option and expose
port 8080. Open the generated Killercoda URL in your browser.

On the Unlock Jenkins page: 1. Paste the initial administrator password.
2. Click Continue. 3. Select Install suggested plugins. 4. Wait for
installation. 5. Create an administrator account and password. 6. Click
Save and Finish, then Start using Jenkins.

PART E – Create Freestyle Job

In Jenkins Dashboard: 1. Click New Item. 2. Item name:
GitHub-Freestyle-Job 3. Select Freestyle project. 4. Click OK.

Source Code Management: Select Git

Repository URL: https://github.com/ro-ro48/devops-external1.git

Branch Specifier: */main

Build Steps: 1. Click Add build step. 2. Select Execute shell. 3. Paste:

    cd set3
    bash freestyle-build.sh

4.  Click Save.

Run the job: 1. Open GitHub-Freestyle-Job. 2. Click Build Now. 3. Open
the latest build number. 4. Click Console Output.

Expected ending:

    5 passed
    Jenkins Freestyle Job Completed Successfully
    Finished: SUCCESS

If Console Output says: cd: can’t cd to set3

Check that the set3/ folder is committed and pushed to GitHub.

If Console Output says: Could not open requirements file:
requirements.txt

Check that set3/requirements.txt exists in GitHub. Create and push it:

    cd /root/set3-devops/devops-external1/set3
    echo "pytest" > requirements.txt
    cd ..
    git add set3/requirements.txt
    git commit -m "Add requirements file for Jenkins"
    git push origin main

Then click Build Now again.

============================================================ QUESTION 2
– JENKINS CI/CD PIPELINE
============================================================

Aim: Implement a Jenkins Pipeline with checkout, dependency
installation, testing, and a demonstration deployment stage.

PART A – Create Jenkinsfile

Where to run: Killercoda terminal, from repository root.

    cd /root/set3-devops/devops-external1

Create set3/Jenkinsfile:

    cat > set3/Jenkinsfile <<'EOF'
    pipeline {
        agent any

        stages {
            stage('Checkout') {
                steps {
                    echo 'Fetching source code from GitHub'
                    checkout scm
                }
            }

            stage('Install Dependencies') {
                steps {
                    dir('set3') {
                        sh '''
                        python3 -m venv venv
                        . venv/bin/activate
                        pip install --upgrade pip
                        pip install -r requirements.txt
                        '''
                    }
                }
            }

            stage('Automated Testing') {
                steps {
                    dir('set3') {
                        sh '''
                        . venv/bin/activate
                        pytest -v --junitxml=test-results.xml
                        '''
                    }
                }
            }

            stage('Deployment') {
                steps {
                    echo 'All tests passed successfully'
                    echo 'Application deployment completed'
                }
            }
        }

        post {
            always {
                junit allowEmptyResults: true,
                      testResults: 'set3/test-results.xml'
            }

            success {
                echo 'CI/CD Pipeline executed successfully'
            }

            failure {
                echo 'CI/CD Pipeline failed'
            }
        }
    }
    EOF

PART B – Commit and push Jenkinsfile

    git add set3/Jenkinsfile
    git commit -m "Add Jenkins CI CD pipeline"
    git push origin main

If nothing to commit, verify that set3/Jenkinsfile is already present in
GitHub.

PART C – Create Pipeline Job in Jenkins

In Jenkins Dashboard: 1. Click New Item. 2. Name: Python-CICD-Pipeline
3. Select Pipeline. 4. Click OK. 5. Scroll to the Pipeline section. 6.
Configure:

