============================================================ QUESTION 3
– GITHUB ACTIONS
============================================================

Aim: Create and execute a GitHub Actions workflow that automatically
runs Python tests.

IMPORTANT: The workflow file must be in the repository-root
.github/workflows/ folder, NOT inside set3/.github/.

PART A – Create workflow directory

Where to run: Killercoda terminal, from repository root.

    cd /root/set3-devops/devops-external1
    mkdir -p .github/workflows

PART B – Create python-ci.yml

    cat > .github/workflows/python-ci.yml <<'EOF'
    name: Python CI Workflow

    on:
      push:
        branches: [main]

      pull_request:
        branches: [main]

    jobs:
      test:
        runs-on: ubuntu-latest

        steps:
          - name: Checkout Repository
            uses: actions/checkout@v4

          - name: Setup Python
            uses: actions/setup-python@v5
            with:
              python-version: '3.12'

          - name: Install Dependencies
            run: |
              cd set3
              python -m pip install --upgrade pip
              pip install -r requirements.txt

          - name: Run Automated Tests
            run: |
              cd set3
              pytest -v
    EOF

PART C – Verify and push workflow

    cat .github/workflows/python-ci.yml
    git add .github/workflows/python-ci.yml
    git commit -m "Add GitHub Actions Python CI workflow"
    git push origin main

If nothing to commit, check that the workflow file is already committed.

PART D – Verify on GitHub website

1.  Open: https://github.com/ro-ro48/devops-external1
2.  Click Actions.
3.  Select Python CI Workflow.
4.  Open the latest workflow run.
5.  Click the test job.
6.  Verify these steps:
    -   Checkout Repository
    -   Setup Python
    -   Install Dependencies
    -   Run Automated Tests

Expected result: 5 passed

A green check mark means the workflow succeeded. If the run is Queued,
wait for it to start before judging the result. If it fails, open the
failed step and inspect its error.

